WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.150
So how do small boats actually capsize?

2
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.775
Almost never from one cause.

3
00:00:06.025 --> 00:00:07.825
It’s a stack of them.

4
00:00:08.075 --> 00:00:10.825
Too much weight, or weight carried too high.

5
00:00:11.075 --> 00:00:14.125
Free surface, and water already in the boat.

6
00:00:14.375 --> 00:00:16.300
A sharp turn at speed.

7
00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:20.250
A following sea that slews her sideways into a broach.

8
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:25.800
And down-flooding: water pouring in through an open hatch, or over a low transom.

9
00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:27.975
What’s the common thread?

10
00:00:28.975 --> 00:00:33.350
The boat was already near her limit before the last wave arrived.

11
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.425
So keep weight low.

12
00:00:35.675 --> 00:00:37.400
Keep water out.

13
00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:39.825
Slow down in rough water.

14
00:00:40.075 --> 00:00:44.500
And keep your bow or your stern, never your beam, toward breaking seas.

15
00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:46.650
That’s lesson five.

16
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:51.175
G pulls down, B pushes up, and G M is your stability.

17
00:00:51.425 --> 00:00:54.200
Snappy is stiff, lazy is tender.

18
00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:56.675
Heel heals, a list lasts.

19
00:00:56.925 --> 00:01:00.900
Next time: hulls, and keeping the water on the outside.
